I see the Treasury's valuation was based on a fixed statutory rate, not the actual market price. The question seems to require taking the physical quantity of silver and multiplying it by an inflation-adjusted market price from each specific time period. This approach would provide a more accurate representation of the silver's value, accounting for both its physical quantity and the market conditions at that time.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_f4deb92d4d514d98a5c76655","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Historical silver prices (nominal) per troy ounce\n# Source: historical data, typically ranging $0.30-$0.90 in 1930s-1950s\n\n# Let me look up approximate historical silver prices\n# 1938: around $0.43-$0.45 per ounce\n# 1948: around $0.70-$0.75 per ounce  \n# 1958: around $0.85-$0.90 per ounce\n\n# Inflation adjustment using CPI-U (from FRED)\n# Base: 2022 dollars\n\n# CPI values (approximate annual average)\ncpi = {\n    1938: 14.1,\n    1948: 24.1,\n    1958: 28.9,\n    2022: 292.7\n}\n\n# Historical silver prices (nominal) - using mid-range estimates\nsilver_prices = {\n    1938: 0.43,  # 1938 average\n    1948: 0.71,  # 1948 average\n    1958: 0.85   # 1958 average\n}\n\n# Calculate inflation-adjusted prices to 2022 dollars\nfor year in [1938, 1948, 1958]:\n    adj_price = silver_prices[year] * (cpi[2022] / cpi[year])\n    print(f\"{year}: Nominal ${silver_prices[year]:.2f}/oz -> Inflation adjusted: ${adj_price:.2f} (2022 dollars)\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
